<!-- 03 PRICE, FUNDING, AND POSITIONING -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Price, Funding, and Positioning</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">The $3,000 reaction, the funding pivot, and how the "stay ahead" message lands</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>The $3,000 Reaction</h2>
    <p>Before the price was revealed, expectations commonly landed in the $1,500 to $3,000 range, so $3,000 is more often described as the higher end of what people expected than as a shock. Judgments of reasonableness are conditional: many call it fair for a university certificate with live instruction, provided the content is proven deep enough. Nearly everyone wants confidence in the return on investment (ROI) before committing, and self-funding is a genuine stretch for lower-income, nonprofit, and individual-contributor participants.</p>
    <div class="q-asked">
      <span class="q-asked-label">Participants were asked, after first naming the price they expected</span>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"This program costs $3,000. What's your reaction to that price? Does it feel worth it for what's offered?"</p>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Share of all 50 participants whose interview raised each theme; themes are counted once per participant. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes.</span></p>
    <div class="bar-chart" data-drilldown-chart="price">
      {sortPct(stats.price as Row[]).map((s) => (
        <div class="bar-row" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="bar-label">{s.code}</span>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill warm" style={`width:${s.pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(s.pct)}%</span></div></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-callout">
      <p><strong>Return on investment (ROI) confidence is the gate, not sticker shock.</strong> 94% said they need confidence in the return before investing, and 86% named the burden of self-funding. Only 70% called $3,000 reasonable outright. The price is defensible, but it is defended by proof of outcomes and by who pays, not by the number itself.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What They Expected It to Cost</h2>
    <p>Every participant was asked to name an expected price before the $3,000 figure was revealed, which gives a clean read on where the price sits against market expectations. The typical expectation centered near {medianExpectation} at the median, and the pattern is one-sided.</p>
    <div class="q-asked">
      <span class="q-asked-label">Participants were asked, before the price was revealed</span>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"Before I tell you, what would you expect a program like this to cost?"</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bar-chart">
      <div class="bar-row">
        <span class="bar-label">Expected less than $3,000</span>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill warm" style={`width:${stats.price_expectations.below_pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(stats.price_expectations.below_pct)}%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <span class="bar-label">$3,000 was the top of their expected range</span>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill warm" style={`width:${stats.price_expectations.at_top_pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(stats.price_expectations.at_top_pct)}%</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="bar-row">
        <span class="bar-label">$3,000 was comfortably within their range</span>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill warm" style={`width:${stats.price_expectations.within_pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(stats.price_expectations.within_pct)}%</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Expectations hand-coded from each participant's stated range (n = {stats.price_expectations.n}). Not clickable; this chart reflects analyst-coded price bands rather than a thematic code.</p>
    <div class="vuln-callout">
      <p><strong>$3,000 sits at or above what 80% of the market expected, and nobody expected more.</strong> The price has no headroom. It is not a launch blocker (70% still judge it reasonable once the university framing is applied), but it rules out any price increase and raises the stakes on proof of value. If enrollment underperforms, the first lever to examine is the funding channel, not a higher or lower sticker price.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>The Funding Pivot</h2>
    <p>If there is one structural finding that should shape the entire enrollment strategy, it is this: enrolling is a funding decision before it is a personal one. Nearly every participant said the source of funding changes how they feel, and a clear majority would prefer their employer to cover or split the cost. A meaningful group anticipates friction getting employer approval, which is where an approval toolkit earns its keep.</p>
    <div class="q-asked">
      <span class="q-asked-label">Participants were asked</span>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"{Q.Q12}"</p>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Share of all 50 participants whose interview raised each theme; themes are counted once per participant. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes.</span></p>
    <div class="bar-chart" data-drilldown-chart="funding">
      {sortPct(stats.funding as Row[]).map((s) => (
        <div class="bar-row" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="bar-label">{s.code}</span>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill signal" style={`width:${s.pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(s.pct)}%</span></div></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="opp-callout">
      <p><strong>Funding is the highest-leverage lever in the study.</strong> 96% said funding source shapes the decision and 88% prefer employer funding. Positioning the program as a manager-approved professional-development investment, complete with a business-case template, converts a $3,000 personal stretch into a routine line item.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>How Contingent Is the Stated Demand?</h3>
    <p>Crossing enrollment intent with funding expectations shows how much of the study's headline demand depends on someone else paying. The answer: nearly all of it.</p>
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">{ceil(100 * stats.intent.Likely / m.n)}%</div><div class="stat-label">Likely to seriously consider enrolling</div></div>
      <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">{fc.hot_employer_pref} of {fc.n_hot}</div><div class="stat-label">Likely enrollees who prefer employer funding</div></div>
      <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">{ceil(fc.hot_funding_shapes_pct)}%</div><div class="stat-label">Of likely enrollees say funding source shapes the decision</div></div>
      <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">{ceil(fc.self_pay_viable_pct)}%</div><div class="stat-label">Of the full sample looks willing and able to self-fund</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-callout">
      <p><strong>The launch case is an employer-channel case.</strong> Only {fc.hot_without_employer_pref} of the {fc.n_hot} likely enrollees expressed intent without also preferring employer funding. Read literally, a launch that relies on individuals paying $3,000 out of pocket addresses roughly {ceil(fc.self_pay_viable_pct)}% of this market. The same intent number supported by an employer-sponsorship channel addresses the full 62%. Enrollment projections and the recruitment budget should be built on the second scenario, with the first as the downside case.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Where the Weight Sits on Price and Barriers</h2>
    <p>The scatter below applies the same intensity lens to the price-and-barriers themes. Themes in the upper-right are both frequently raised and heavily weighted, marking the objections that most deserve a direct answer.</p>
    <div class="scatter-wrap">
      <svg class="scatter-svg" viewBox={`0 0 ${scBarriers.W} ${scBarriers.H}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Frequency by intensity scatter, barriers and price sensitivity">
        {scBarriers.xTicks.map((v) => (
          <>
            <line x1={scBarriers.xOf(v)} y1={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB} x2={scBarriers.xOf(v)} y2={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB + 4} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.35)" />
            <text x={scBarriers.xOf(v)} y={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB + 16} text-anchor="middle" class="sc-tick">{v}%</text>
          </>
        ))}
        {scBarriers.yTicks.map((v) => (
          <>
            <line x1={scBarriers.padL - 4} y1={scBarriers.yOf(v)} x2={scBarriers.padL} y2={scBarriers.yOf(v)} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.35)" />
            <text x={scBarriers.padL - 8} y={scBarriers.yOf(v) + 3} text-anchor="end" class="sc-tick">{scBarriers.fmtY(v)}</text>
          </>
        ))}
        <line x1={scBarriers.padL} y1={scBarriers.yOf(scBarriers.yMed)} x2={scBarriers.W - scBarriers.padR} y2={scBarriers.yOf(scBarriers.yMed)} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.18)" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
        <line x1={scBarriers.xOf(scBarriers.xMed)} y1={scBarriers.padT} x2={scBarriers.xOf(scBarriers.xMed)} y2={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.18)" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
        <line x1={scBarriers.padL} y1={scBarriers.padT} x2={scBarriers.padL} y2={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.25)" />
        <line x1={scBarriers.padL} y1={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB} x2={scBarriers.W - scBarriers.padR} y2={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.25)" />
        <text x={scBarriers.padL + scBarriers.plotW / 2} y={scBarriers.H - 12} text-anchor="middle" class="sc-axis">How often the theme came up (% of participants)</text>
        <text x={16} y={scBarriers.padT + scBarriers.plotH / 2} text-anchor="middle" class="sc-axis" transform={`rotate(-90 16 ${scBarriers.padT + scBarriers.plotH / 2})`}>Mean intensity (BWS)</text>
        <text x={scBarriers.W - scBarriers.padR - 6} y={scBarriers.padT + 14} text-anchor="end" class="sc-quad">Address before launch</text>
        <text x={scBarriers.padL + 6} y={scBarriers.padT + 14} class="sc-quad">Acute for a few</text>
        <text x={scBarriers.W - scBarriers.padR - 6} y={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB - 8} text-anchor="end" class="sc-quad">Common but mild</text>
        <text x={scBarriers.padL + 6} y={scBarriers.H - scBarriers.padB - 8} class="sc-quad">Background</text>
        {scBarriers.pts.map((p) => (
          <circle cx={scBarriers.xOf(p.freq_pct)} cy={scBarriers.yOf(p.mean_bws)} r="6" fill="#B8341B" fill-opacity="0.72" />
        ))}
        {scBarriers.labels.map((l) => (
          <>
            <line x1={l.cx + 3} y1={l.cy} x2={l.lx - 2} y2={l.ly - 3} stroke="rgba(11,31,58,0.20)" stroke-width="1" />
            <text x={l.lx} y={l.ly} class="sc-label">{l.label}</text>
          </>
        ))}
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Price-and-barrier themes pooled from the two price questions and the funding question quoted in this section, plus the two barrier questions quoted in the Barriers section. Horizontal position is prevalence; vertical position is mean Best-Worst Scaling intensity. Dashed lines mark the medians of the plotted set. Best-Worst Scaling reliability for this grouping: Krippendorff ordinal alpha = 0.81 (n = 50). Showing themes raised by at least a quarter of participants.</p>

    <h2>How the "Stay Ahead" Message Lands</h2>
    <p>The concept was pitched as a way to "stay ahead as AI reshapes your field, rather than get left behind." That framing works, but it works unevenly, and the pattern matters for the lead recruitment message. Fear-of-being-left-behind resonates with people already leaning in. The professionals still on the fence, the audience the recruitment effort actually needs to move, are noticeably more likely to push back on it and to prefer an opportunity or productivity angle.</p>
    <div class="q-asked">
      <span class="q-asked-label">Participants were asked</span>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"{Q.Q10}"</p>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Share of all 50 participants whose interview raised each theme; themes are counted once per participant. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes.</span></p>
    <div class="bar-chart" data-drilldown-chart="positioning">
      {sortPct(stats.positioning as Row[]).map((s) => (
        <div class="bar-row" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="bar-label">{s.code}</span>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill ink" style={`width:${s.pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(s.pct)}%</span></div></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>Fear framing preaches to the choir.</strong> It resonates with the likely cohort (87%) but the unsure cohort resists it at 50% versus 23% of the likely cohort, a statistically significant gap. Opportunity framing (68% overall) and a "make me more effective" angle are the safer lead for the persuadable middle. Save urgency for audiences already leaning in.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 04 THE INTENT COHORTS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">The Intent Cohorts</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">Who leans in, who is on the fence, and where the two diverge</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <p>Rather than impose a statistical clustering on a sample of this size, we group participants by their stated likelihood of enrolling. That intent measure is the study's outcome variable, so grouping on it gives a direct read on what separates buyers from fence-sitters. Three cohorts emerge.</p>

    <div class="scorecard" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;">
      <div class="scorecard-header">Likely (hot)</div>
      <div class="scorecard-header">Unsure (warm)</div>
      <div class="scorecard-header">Unlikely (cold)</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell win"><strong>{stats.intent.Likely} of {m.n}</strong> &middot; {ceil(100 * stats.intent.Likely / m.n)}%</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell"><strong>{stats.intent.Unsure} of {m.n}</strong> &middot; {ceil(100 * stats.intent.Unsure / m.n)}%</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell lose"><strong>{stats.intent.Unlikely} of {m.n}</strong> &middot; {ceil(100 * stats.intent.Unlikely / m.n)}%</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell">Sold on the concept and the price; need a funding path and a nudge.</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell">Like the concept; need proof of depth, level fit, and flexibility.</div>
      <div class="scorecard-cell">Single participant; not interpreted.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="seg-note">
      <p>The unlikely cohort is a single participant. We report the count for completeness but draw no conclusions from it. All cohort comparisons below are between the likely cohort (n = {stats.meta.n_hot}) and the unsure cohort (n = {stats.meta.n_warm}). At these sample sizes, only differences that pass a significance test are treated as real; everything else is read as directional at most.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Where Likely and Unsure Diverge</h2>
    <p>The two cohorts part ways on conviction: the likely cohort finds the price reasonable, feels a personal AI gap, and treats structure as clearly worth paying for. The unsure cohort withholds on price, wants proof the content is not too basic, and pushes back on fear-based urgency. Unlike the single-question charts earlier in the report, these themes aggregate each participant's full interview, so no single question is quoted above the chart; click any theme to read the underlying quotes, each shown with the verbatim question that prompted it. The first chart shows only the gaps that pass a two-proportion significance test at the 0.10 level (marked with an asterisk); the second shows the themes where the two cohorts broadly agree.</p>

    <div class="paired-legend"><span class="pl-swatch pl-hot"></span> Likely (n = {stats.meta.n_hot}) &nbsp;&nbsp; <span class="pl-swatch pl-warm"></span> Unsure (n = {stats.meta.n_warm})</div>

    <h3>The Significant Gaps</h3>
    <div class="paired-chart" data-drilldown-chart="cohort_gaps">
      {sigRows.map((s) => (
        <div class="paired-block" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="paired-label">{s.code}<span class="sig-star">*</span></span>
          <div class="paired-bars">
            <div class="pb-track"><div class="pb-fill pl-hot" style={`width:${s.hot_pct}%`}><span class="pb-val">{ceil(s.hot_pct)}%</span></div></div>
            <div class="pb-track"><div class="pb-fill pl-warm" style={`width:${s.warm_pct}%`}><span class="pb-val">{ceil(s.warm_pct)}%</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Themes where the likely-versus-unsure difference is individually significant at alpha = 0.10, two-tailed (two-proportion z-test, with Fisher's exact test where expected cell counts fall below five). Sorted by gap size. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes with the question that prompted each.</span></p>

    <h3>Where They Broadly Agree</h3>
    <div class="paired-chart" data-drilldown-chart="cohort_gaps">
      {agreeRows.map((s) => (
        <div class="paired-block" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="paired-label">{s.code}</span>
          <div class="paired-bars">
            <div class="pb-track"><div class="pb-fill pl-hot" style={`width:${s.hot_pct}%`}><span class="pb-val">{ceil(s.hot_pct)}%</span></div></div>
            <div class="pb-track"><div class="pb-fill pl-warm" style={`width:${s.warm_pct}%`}><span class="pb-val">{ceil(s.warm_pct)}%</span></div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Themes where the cohort difference does not reach significance at alpha = 0.10; both cohorts give the concept a warm reception, need ROI confidence, and prefer employer funding. Sorted by prevalence in the likely cohort. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes.</span></p>

    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>The likely cohort buys the value story; the unsure cohort has not been given enough proof yet.</strong> Significant gaps favor the likely cohort on finding the price reasonable (84% versus 50%), valuing structure over free content (94% versus 72%), seeing free content as limited (100% versus 78%), and feeling a personal AI gap (77% versus 50%). The unsure cohort significantly over-indexes only on resisting fear framing and on wanting an advanced track, both of which are addressable by message and product changes rather than discounting.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Is in Each Cohort</h2>
    <p>The cohorts are not cleanly split by AI experience. The unsure cohort is bimodal: it holds both a larger share of non-users (who need reassurance the program is not too advanced) and the experienced users who worry it is too basic. The likely cohort is anchored by the occasional-user middle, the group for whom a structured push is exactly what they have been missing. The depth concern maps almost perfectly onto experience: 95% of regular AI users worry the content is too basic, versus 40% of occasional users and 9% of non-users (the Program Design section quantifies this and sizes the advanced-track case).</p>
    <div class="crosstab">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th style="text-align:left">Current AI use</th><th>Likely cohort</th><th>Unsure cohort</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {aiOrder.map((k) => (
            <tr>
              <td style="text-align:left">{k}</td>
              <td style="text-align:center">{aiHot[k] ?? 0} ({ceil(100 * (aiHot[k] ?? 0) / stats.meta.n_hot)}%)</td>
              <td style="text-align:center">{aiWarm[k] ?? 0} ({ceil(100 * (aiWarm[k] ?? 0) / stats.meta.n_warm)}%)</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Current AI use by intent cohort. Likely cohort n = {stats.meta.n_hot}; unsure cohort n = {stats.meta.n_warm}.</p>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 05 PROGRAM POSITIONING AND RECRUITMENT -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Program Positioning and Recruitment</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">The lead message, who to recruit first, and what to avoid saying</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>Positioning Recommendation</h2>
    <div class="opp-callout">
      <p><strong>Lead with opportunity, not fear.</strong> Anchor the lead message on becoming demonstrably more effective at your actual job with no coding required. Opportunity framing is preferred by 68% and, critically, does not repel the persuadable unsure cohort the way "get left behind" does. Reserve urgency and "stay ahead" language for audiences who have already shown intent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vuln-callout">
      <p><strong>Message that backfires:</strong> a fear-first headline. The undecided cohort resists it (50% versus 23% of the likely cohort, a significant gap). It converts people who were already going to convert and alienates the ones you need to persuade.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who to Recruit First</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Audience</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Primary</td><td>Occasional AI users in mid-career knowledge-work roles</td><td>They anchor the likely cohort. They feel a real gap, have tried and abandoned free content, and respond to a structured, guided path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Secondary</td><td>Managers and team leads who buy for their people</td><td>Many framed the program as valuable for their team rather than only themselves. 44% preferred a team-leadership framing, which doubles as an employer-funded entry point.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nurture</td><td>The unsure cohort</td><td>Not lost. Move them with a published curriculum, proof of outcomes, and payment flexibility rather than urgency.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Message Pillars</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Evidence</th><th>How to say it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Built for non-technical professionals</td><td>78% cite the no-coding hook</td><td>"No coding. No computer-science background. Just your real work, done better with AI."</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Structure beats scattered free content</td><td>86% value structure; 92% cite free-content limits</td><td>"You have watched the free videos. This is the guided path that actually gets you there, with a cohort and a finish line."</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A credential from a university you know</td><td>54% say the university signals rigor</td><td>Use the credential as a proof point and an employer-justification asset, not the lead promise.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 06 PROGRAM DESIGN AND CURRICULUM -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Program Design and Curriculum</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">Level fit, curriculum transparency, and format flexibility</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>Close the Level-Fit Gap</h2>
    <p>The most common depth concern is that the program will be too basic for experienced users: 52% of the full sample raised it. Splitting that concern by how much each participant already uses AI shows it is not a diffuse worry. It is concentrated almost entirely among the market's most experienced segment.</p>
    <div class="paired-legend">Share raising the "too basic for advanced users" concern, by current AI use</div>
    <div class="bar-chart">
      {stats.too_basic_by_familiarity.rows.map((r) => (
        <div class="bar-row">
          <span class="bar-label">{r.label} (n = {r.n})</span>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill plum" style={`width:${r.too_basic_pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(r.too_basic_pct)}%</span></div></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Share of each experience group raising the "content too basic for advanced users" concern anywhere in their interview. The regular-user rate is significantly higher than the rest of the sample combined (alpha = 0.10, two-tailed; the difference is significant well beyond that threshold). Not clickable by group; the theme's quotes are available from the barriers chart.</p>
    <div class="vuln-callout">
      <p><strong>The advanced-track question is a regular-user question, and regular users are 36% of this market.</strong> 95% of regular AI users worry the content is too basic for them, versus 40% of occasional users and 9% of non-users, a statistically significant concentration. Five of the eighteen regular users went further and proposed a concrete mechanism (an advanced track, tier, or placement diagnostic). A published level-fit mechanism, whether a placement diagnostic, a labeled advanced track, or clear prerequisites, turns the single most common objection into a reason to trust the program, and it defines exactly who the second-cohort or advanced offering should serve.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Publish the Curriculum</h2>
    <p>A quarter of participants (26%) said a detailed, publicly visible curriculum breakdown is a precondition for enrolling. This is a low-cost, high-trust asset: a week-by-week syllabus that shows the depth, the tools covered, and the outputs each learner will produce. It answers the depth question, the level-fit question, and the employer-justification question at once.</p>

    <h2>Add Format Flexibility Without Losing the Cohort</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Record every live session</td><td>58% asked for session recordings</td><td>Removes the biggest format objection. A missed evening should never mean falling behind or losing the sunk cost.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Publish cohort dates well ahead</td><td>56% flagged unpredictable work demands; 34% named seasonal conflicts</td><td>Lets professionals in tax, healthcare, and public-sector cycles pick a cohort that avoids their crunch.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offer sector-relevant project options</td><td>58% wanted content tied to their function or sector</td><td>The own-work framing is the top appeal driver. Sector-tailored project prompts deepen it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 07 ENROLLMENT STRATEGY AND EMPLOYER PARTNERSHIPS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Enrollment Strategy and Employer Partnerships</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">Recruit through the employer, de-risk the price, and equip the internal champion</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>Build Enrollment Around Employer Sponsorship</h2>
    <div class="opp-callout">
      <p><strong>Enrolling is a funding decision first.</strong> 96% said who pays shapes it, 88% prefer employer funding, and 27 of the 31 likely enrollees tie their intent to employer support. The default enrollment path should be an employer-sponsored one, with a self-pay option as the exception rather than the headline. This single structural choice does more for enrollment than any price change.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Equip the Internal Champion</h2>
    <p>Over half of participants (54%) anticipated friction getting employer approval. The people who want the program are often not the people who control the budget. Give the would-be learner a ready-made business case: a one-page justification with the curriculum, the outcomes, the credential, and a cost-versus-value framing they can forward to a manager. This directly addresses approval friction and the toolkit that a fifth of participants explicitly asked for.</p>

    <h2>De-Risk the Price</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Prove the return</td><td>94% need confidence in the return before investing</td><td>Lead with outcomes: what graduates can do afterward, with concrete before-and-after work examples. Publish alumni results as they accrue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offer payment plans</td><td>Payment-plan demand runs 28% in the unsure cohort versus 6% in the likely cohort</td><td>A monthly plan meaningfully lowers the barrier for self-funders and the fence-sitters, without touching the headline price.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hold the price, sell the value</td><td>70% already find $3,000 reasonable; expectations clustered at $1,500 to $3,000</td><td>The number is defensible. Do not discount broadly. Defend the price with proof and funding pathways instead.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 08 BARRIERS, RISK, AND PRE-LAUNCH VALIDATION -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Barriers, Risk, and Pre-Launch Validation</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">What stops enrollment, who owns each fix, and what a pilot should prove</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>The Barrier Landscape</h2>
    <p>Asked directly what would stop them from enrolling and what single change would move them, participants named a consistent set of barriers. Cost and the confidence to justify it dominate, followed by depth and level-fit concerns and the practical friction of fitting a live cohort around unpredictable work.</p>
    <div class="q-asked">
      <span class="q-asked-label">Participants were asked two questions</span>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"{Q.Q13}"</p>
      <p class="q-asked-text">"{Q.Q15}"</p>
    </div>
    <p class="source-note">Share of all 50 participants whose interview raised each theme; themes are counted once per participant. <span class="vb-hint">Click a theme to read participant quotes.</span></p>
    <div class="bar-chart" data-drilldown-chart="barriers">
      {sortPct(stats.barriers as Row[]).map((s) => (
        <div class="bar-row" data-code={s.code}>
          <span class="bar-label">{s.code}</span>
          <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill signal" style={`width:${s.pct}%`}><span class="bar-val">{ceil(s.pct)}%</span></div></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2>Barrier-to-Fix Map</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Barrier</th><th>Prevalence</th><th>Fix already in this report</th><th>Owner</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Self-funded cost burden</td><td>86%</td><td>Employer-sponsorship pathway and payment plans.</td><td>Corporate partnerships</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Time and the confidence to justify it</td><td>76% name time as the primary learning barrier</td><td>Recorded sessions, dates published ahead, and a business case for the employer.</td><td>Program design</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Program-quality and depth uncertainty</td><td>60%</td><td>Published curriculum and outcome proof.</td><td>Program design, Faculty</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sector-specific relevance</td><td>58% want function or sector fit</td><td>Sector-tailored project options.</td><td>Faculty</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Too basic for experienced users</td><td>52% (95% of regular AI users)</td><td>Advanced or intermediate track and a placement diagnostic.</td><td>Program design, Faculty</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="seg-note">
      <p>Every high-prevalence barrier in this study maps to a change the program can make, not to a flaw in the concept itself. The risk is not that professionals dislike the offering. The risk is friction: an unclear return, an approval process without a champion's toolkit, and a depth question left unanswered. Each is addressable before launch.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What to Validate Before a Full Launch</h2>
    <p>Concept-test findings tell you what the market says it will do; a pilot cohort tells you what it actually does. The standard path from this evidence to a full launch is a single pilot cohort, ideally filled through two or three employer partners, which tests the sponsorship channel and the program in the same pass. Four things are worth measuring deliberately.</p>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>What to validate</th><th>How</th><th>Decision it informs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Real conversion from stated intent</td><td>Track how many expressions of interest become paid enrollments in the pilot. Stated intent in concept tests overstates purchase, so the pilot's conversion rate becomes the planning number for cohort-size and revenue projections.</td><td>Full-launch enrollment targets and recruitment budget.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The employer channel</td><td>Fill part of the pilot through employer partners using the approval toolkit. Measure how long approvals take and where they stall.</td><td>Whether corporate partnerships or individual recruitment leads the full launch.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Self-pay demand</td><td>Keep an individual enrollment path open during the pilot and count uptake, including payment-plan usage.</td><td>Whether the 8% self-pay estimate holds, and whether payment plans move it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Advanced-track demand</td><td>Include a placement diagnostic at pilot intake and record how many entrants score above the core curriculum's level.</td><td>Whether to build the advanced track for the second cohort or defer it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- 09 METHODOLOGY -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <div style="flex:1;">
      <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:16px;">
        <div style="flex:1;">
          <h2 class="accordion-title">Methodology</h2>
          <p class="accordion-subtitle">Sample, statistical conventions, coding and reliability, and known gaps</p>
        </div>
        <span class="accordion-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h3>Sample design</h3>
    <p>This is a concept-acceptance study. Fifty working professionals in the Greater Toronto Area, all currently employed with at least six years of experience and aged 25 or older, took part in a structured 15-question interview. Each reacted to a described continuing-education certificate: an eight-week online university program teaching non-technical professionals to use generative AI in their daily work, one live evening a week plus about four to five hours total, hands-on with real tools, ending in a university professional certificate. The concept was presented without a price; the $3,000 price was revealed partway through the interview. There was no company-size or seniority screen, so the sample spans individual contributors through senior leaders and small organizations through large enterprises, with a deliberate spread of AI familiarity (regular, occasional, and non-users).</p>

    <h3>Statistical methodology</h3>
    <p>All significance tests use an alpha of 0.10, two-tailed. Comparisons between the likely and unsure cohorts use a two-proportion z-test where all expected cell counts reach five, with Fisher's exact test as the fallback for smaller cells. At a sample of 50, with cohorts of {stats.meta.n_hot} and {stats.meta.n_warm}, only differences that pass this test are reported as real. Non-significant differences are described as directional at most, and uncorroborated non-significant differences are not claimed. All percentages are rounded up to the nearest whole number.</p>

    <h3>Coding and reliability</h3>
    <p>Every interview was coded on three dimensions: a thematic code, a direction (positive, negative, or neutral) per meaning unit, and a Best-Worst Scaling (BWS) intensity score that captures how heavily each theme was weighted within a question grouping. Thematic and direction coding used two independent coders with a third resolving disagreements. Agreement was almost perfect: Cohen's kappa of 0.905 on thematic codes and 0.934 on direction. Intensity used a two-stage Best-Worst Scaling design (identifying each participant's peak mention, then scaling those peaks), with reliability reported per question grouping. Krippendorff's ordinal alpha ran from 0.70 to 0.84 across the four groupings (0.84 for concept appeal, 0.81 for barriers and price, 0.70 for both program evaluation and AI readiness), with Spearman rank correlations between coders in the same range. The two scatter plots in this report draw on the two most reliable groupings.</p>
    <p>Where a reception claim pairs a prevalence figure with an "affirmed outright" figure (for example, the weekly time commitment), the first counts everyone who raised the theme and the second counts only those whose statements carried a positive direction; the gap between them is the share who engaged with the theme conditionally. One analysis is analyst-coded rather than pipeline-coded: pre-reveal price expectations were hand-classified from each participant's stated expectation range into three bands relative to the $3,000 price (entirely below it, topping out at it, or comfortably containing it), following the participant's own characterization where one was given.</p>

    <h3>Known gaps</h3>
    <p>Stated intent overstates real enrollment: nothing was actually purchased, so the 62% "likely" figure is a relative signal, not a conversion forecast, and it is expected to compress in a larger and more representative sample. Segments here are intent cohorts, not a formal statistical segmentation, which a sample of this size cannot reliably support. The unlikely cohort is a single participant and is not interpreted. The sample is limited to the Greater Toronto Area and to employed professionals with six or more years of experience, so findings should not be generalized to students, early-career workers, or other regions without further research.</p>

  </div>
</details>

</div>

